how much is university going to cost me  so there are two components to expense  at university you have your tuition fees  and your living costs or maintenance  your tuition fees will be nine thousand  pounds per year if you're applying from  the UK and they can be between fifteen  and twenty one thousand pounds per year  if you're applying from overseas though  this can be higher for clinical medicine  these fees aren't all payable upfront  and you can get a loan to cover them  from the UK government if you are  applying from the first undergraduate  degree and from the UK or the EU more  information about that here and note the  Oxford also provides reduced tuition  fees for students based on household  income so if you're from a low income  family then you can save up to three  thousand pounds per year on your tuition  fees more low information about that  scheme here now when it comes to living  costs this will obviously vary a lot  from person to person based on your  spending habits but generally you can  expect to spend between one thousand and  one and a half thousand pounds per month  for everything for the six months that  you'll be at university both Oxford and  Cambridge provide estimates for living  costs at the universities which can be  found here and here and again a loan is  available from the UK governments to  cover these costs more information about  which can be found here secondly what  kind of financial assistance is  available now if you are a UK student  then in addition to applying for a  tuition fee loan from the student loans  company you are also eligible to receive  funding to your living costs and the  amount available to you will vary  depending on where you're from in the UK  regardless of household income you can  apply for a maintenance loan and if  you're from a low income background you  can also apply for a non repayable  maintenance grant information about this  can be found here in terms of financial  support both universities make it their  mission that no student should have to  decline and offer to study there because  of financial reasons Oxford has a  fantastically generous bursary scheme  offering up to four and a half thousand  pounds per year in non repayable grants  based on household income in addition to  this there are other scholarships and  grants available for more information  you can check out these two links while  cambridge offers a very generous bursary  scheme as well offering up to ten  thousand pounds over three years again  assessed based on household income more  information about that check out this  link lastly it's worth noting that  neither Oxford or Cambridge allow you to  work whilst you're at university  with the exception of some bar shifts in  your college and frankly even if you  wanted to work you probably wouldn't  have time to the eight-week terms are  incredibly intense and if you were in  the position where you needed financial  support from a job to continue studying  then funds would be available to you  from the University also remember that  the short terms mean that you have a lot  of time spend at home where you can take  on part-time employment to support  yourself though again if this is  necessary of you to continue studying  then funds will be available to you  either from the University or from your  college
 

